WEBVTT

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.820
Nodoka!

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.250
<i>This is a dream, right?</i>

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.550
<i>We're the same age,</i>

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.840
<i>but Shizuno is wearing her 
middle-school uniform and Ako</i>

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.260
<i>has her grade-school book bag,</i>

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.740
<i>and I'm wearing my high-school uniform.</i>

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.330
<i>So why...</i>

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.280
<i>That was a dream.</i>

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.280
Why now?

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.000
<b>Round 10:
Dealer's Streak</b>

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.450
It's the seventh day of the Inter-high tournament,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.170
the Block A semifinals.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.170
The first players' match is finally in 
South Hand 3 of the first half.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.170
National High School
Mahjong Championship
Day 7 - Block A
 Semifinals

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.930
<i>Matsumi Kuro from Achiga is the dealer.</i>

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.650
Shindouji's Hanada Kirame 
successfully put a stop

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.580
to the champion's winning streak

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.470
in South Hand 1, but again in Hand 2,

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.660
Miyanaga had a 2,600 point win.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.940
Could this be the start of another streak?

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.690
South Hand 3

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.690
Dealer: Matsumi Kuro

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.670
Senriyama Girls' (North Osaka)
Onjouji Toki

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:00.680
Shiraitodai (West Tokyo)
Miyanaga Teru

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.680
Shindouji Girls' (Fukuoka)
Hanada Kirame

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.500
<i>The first half is about to end,</i>

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.680
Achiga Girls' (Nara)
Matsumi Kuro

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.330
<i>but I haven't won even once.</i>

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.710
<i>Am I going to be a burden 
to everybody again?</i>

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.340
<i>We practiced so hard...</i>

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.920
<i>We all gave our best.</i>

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:26.270
<i>Maybe it was just impossible 
to get better in one day.</i>

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.310
<i>I have to do something while I'm dealer!</i>

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.700
<i>I knew it. Not a tile that can be called.</i>

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.950
<i>If nobody calls this, then...</i>

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.210
<i>she'll take it again.</i>

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.780
Tsumo.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.480
1,300. 2,600.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:06.970
<i>Huh?</i>

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.980
<i>She could've won from the
9 of characters I threw away earlier.</i>

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.980
<i>Why didn't she?</i>

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.550
Kuro and the champion might be the same.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.030
Huh? What do you mean?

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:27.330
In the past, Miyanaga Teru often 
ignored a potential win, just like now,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.230
but then always went on to win 
with a hand worth even more.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.440
That's why people thought it was

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.440
just her confidence as a champion.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.840
What if it wasn't really confidence,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.460
but actually her weak point?

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.450
Like how Kuro can't discard dora...

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.530
Yes! Because we have a friend like Kuro,

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.640
we can believe in the possibility.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.370
First Players' Match - First Half
South Hand 4

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.370
Dealer: Onjouji Toki

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:17.750
Riichi.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.900
We're in the first half's last hand!

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.260
The champion is trying to extend 
her lead with a double riichi!

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.260
[double riichi - riichi in the first turn]

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.790
<i>I'm dealer for this last hand,</i>

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.350
<i>but it doesn't look like 
this will go anywhere.</i>

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:33.100
<i>There are zero safe tiles.</i>

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:37.100
Well, if this one's it, 
then it's a total accident!

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.060
Go!

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:39.860
Ron.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.440
8,000.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:45.730
Marvelous!

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.160
That's the end of the first half!

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.920
Shiraitodai (West Tokyo)
Miyanaga Teru

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.190
The first half of this first players' match

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.650
was a one-sided affair for 
West Tokyo's representative,

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.460
Shiraitodai High School.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.650
In the end, being the 
only ones to gain points,

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.360
Senriyama Girls' (North Osaka)
Onjouji Toki

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:11.700
they have an over 50,000 point lead over the

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.180
Achiga Girls' (Nara)
Matsumi Kuro

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.400
second place North Tokyo's Senriyama Girls'.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.640
Shindouji Girls' (Fukuoka)
Hanada Kirame

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.490
Can the other three schools

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.130
possibly make a comeback 
in the second half?!

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.220
Shiraitodai's Miyanaga generally

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.900
didn't get very expensive hands,

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.950
but the number of hands she 
won is very impressive.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:31.950
She had one for 18,000 points, 
you're saying that's low?

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.640
Oh... I meant compared to 
last year's Amae Koromo,

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.500
or other high power 
players of that caliber.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.110
That's so typical of 
the pro-player Kokaji!

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.560
The best player in Japan won't 
give young girls a break.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.130
It has nothing to do 
with them being young!

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:48.620
Please don't flatter me in 
that kind of unpleasant way.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.870
Kuro-san!

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.340
Guys...

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.250
I lost us a lot of points, again.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:20.780
But you put up a good fight!

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:21.600
She's right!

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.550
You faced a stronger player 
than in your second match.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.620
You lost fewer points than in 
the second round match despite

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.300
facing stronger opponents, 
including the champion.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.050
Compared to the stats of 
Miyanaga Teru's previous opponents,

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.590
you put up a good fight.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.050
Have some confidence!

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.430
How can I have any confidence 
when I haven't won a single hand?

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.310
We still have the second 
half of this match!

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.900
Kuro-chan, I brought you a drink.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:43.790
Thanks, onee-chan.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.980
You know, we noticed something 
about Miyanaga Teru.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.220
You mean how she keeps 
the hand values low?

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.990
You noticed, too?

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:58.950
I got a feeling, yes.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.530
It seemed kind of like my limitation

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.840
of never throwing away a dora.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.150
If that's true,

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.340
then there must be times when 
it works to her disadvantage.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:10.960
I'll try my hardest!

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.260
The match will resume shortly.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:14.990
We ask that each school's representative

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.540
please come to the 
tournament hall at this time.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:18.310
All right.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:19.720
Here I go!

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:20.980
Yeah!

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.490
Give it your best!

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:23.750
Fight!

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.340
Toki, you've got to wake up now.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.210
It's hard to go back out there.

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:43.960
South  North  East  West

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:53.260
The second half of the first 
match of the semifinals...

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.640
...starts now!

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.060
The first dealer is Shindouji's 
Hanada Kirame.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:06.180
Which means Miyanaga is the last dealer?

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.070
Looks like the seatings for the 
second half are a little dangerous.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.630
Meaning?

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.510
Being the last dealer synergizes the best

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:16.950
with Miyanaga's winning streaks.

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.840
Ah, you're talking 
about an ideal situation

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.760
where she gets the dealer's 
bonus after winning

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.500
three increasingly valuable hands in a row.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.220
Yes, and since she is the last dealer,

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.800
she can do it twice.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.960
<i>Miyanaga only became the 
team's first player after</i>

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.460
<i>this year's Inter-High preliminaries.</i>

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.100
<i>The only first players' match at the West Tokyo preliminaries that ended with a player</i>

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.760
<i>running out of points was in this formation.</i>

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.900
<i>Shiraitodai High School decided to 
make Miyanaga their first player</i>

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:43.930
<i>after she showed her 
strength as the last dealer.</i>

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:49.310
Tsumo.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:50.920
300, 500.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:52.780
Ouch, dealer woes...

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:54.280
<i>She's fast.</i>

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.160
Senpai, I'm going to put it here.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.180
Is Assam tea all right?

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:08.300
That's early morning tea...

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.220
Wait, so that means it's morning, right?

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.460
Will you turn the TV on?

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.190
Are you sure that's a good idea?

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.750
I told you to do it, so it's fine!

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:20.970
Okay, okay.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.850
<i>That's her second consecutive win!</i>

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:25.930
It's Miyanaga Teru.

00:10:26.270 --> 00:10:27.570
Right now...

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.800
It was East Hand 2 of the second half,

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.360
and the champion just won 
a 500, 1,000 point hand.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:34.190
Got it.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.490
Wait, she's already 
leading by that much?!

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.700
But the second half of 
the match just started!

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.200
<i>Onjouji Toki was 50,000 points 
ahead of me when I played her,</i>

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.660
<i>and she's already 70,000 
points ahead of her?!</i>

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.530
This is too scary!

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:53.790
It looks like she's going 
to keep increasing her lead.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.950
She's out of our league.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.130
East Hand 3

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:01.050
Pon.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.190
Tsumo.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.120
1000, 2000.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.640
<i>Even though we could shift 
the order now and then,</i>

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.280
<i>she still ended up winning.</i>

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.290
<i>I guess seeing ahead 
one turn isn't enough.</i>

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.550
<i>This is the first time I've 
faced someone like this.</i>

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:32.750
East Hand 4

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:32.750
Dealer: Miyanaga Teru

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:38.020
Now the champion is dealer 
with three consecutive wins.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.720
This is not an easy situation 
for the other three schools.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.580
Says pro player Kokaji with a smug look!

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.970
It's too bad our viewers can't see it.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.100
I'm not looking smug at all!

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.970
<i>I'm feeling an exhaustion 
that doesn't even compare</i>

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:56.360
<i>to what I felt in the second round match.</i>

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.140
<i>I guess this is the power 
of an Inter-high Champion.</i>

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.580
<i>We've got to get her out of 
the dealer's seat quickly.</i>

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:09.910
Chi!

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:18.640
<i>Maybe here?</i>

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:22.970
Pon!

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:24.220
<i>Good.</i>

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:26.710
<i>My hand is too slow.</i>

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:29.220
<i>This has to be fast, so I'll 
have Shindouji win this hand.</i>

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.610
<i>It didn't work?</i>

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:43.620
Ron.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:46.890
5,800.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.450
<i>Her hands are getting 
progressively more expensive again.</i>

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:54.870
<i>Here we go.</i>

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:57.460
<i>The champion's started her 
winning streak as dealer.</i>

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:18.940
It's bonus hand 1 of East Hand 4.

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.200
<i>So, who will be the one to put a stop</i>

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:23.570
<i>to the champion Miyanaga 
Teru's winning streak?</i>

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.520
<i>She'll win the next turn?</i>

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:44.250
<i>She's already in tenpai?</i>

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:49.670
<i>I can't call that tile to shift the order.</i>

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:00.650
Tsumo.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:03.730
2,700 from all.

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:06.280
Marvelous...

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:08.870
The champion is on a dealer's streak!

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:10.500
With a tsumo, pinfu, sanshoku,

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:10.500
[Pinfu - A hand in which no bonus fu points are awarded]

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.190
it's a 2,600 base that becomes 
a 2,700-from-all win!

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:14.790
And it becomes a second bonus hand!

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:18.630
<i>With each consecutive win as the dealer,</i>

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.260
<i>each additional bonus would 
increase her winnings</i>

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.720
<i>even with the same hand.</i>

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.920
<i>But Miyanaga Teru does not rely on that.</i>

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:29.250
<i>Which means her next win will be</i>

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.840
<i>at least 9,300 points.</i>

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.460
<i>Anything beyond that is lethal.</i>

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:33.930
<i>I'm putting a stop to this.</i>

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:36.640
East Hand 4, Bonus 2

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:50.700
Kan.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:54.360
<i>Kan.</i>

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.840
<i>Wait, that's what Funa-Q said.</i>

00:14:56.840 --> 00:15:01.980
<i>The Nagano player who beat Amae Koromo 
was like a relative to Miyanaga Teru.</i>

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:04.500
<i>Her specialty was...</i>

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:11.680
Riichi.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.840
<i>So this time it's a riichi.</i>

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:15.430
Chi!

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:20.190
<i>It looks like you're having a harder 
time raising your hand's value.</i>

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.040
<i>I guess there's no way she 
could get a rinshan kaihou,</i>

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.040
[Rinshan kaihou - winning off the extra tile you get from a kan]

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.350
<i>but a closed kan means she has 
at least 50 fu, guaranteed.</i>

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:30.150
<i>Combined with the riichi, if she 
has at least two han, she's set.</i>

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.730
<i>And if she gets kan-dora...</i>

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.170
<i>Kan-dora...</i>

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.420
<i>Looks like her hand is ridiculous.</i>

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.460
<i>I've got all these doras,</i>

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.780
<i>but my hand isn't getting anywhere!</i>

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:59.800
<i>Plus, all the non-dora 
tiles feel dangerous!</i>

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.330
<i>Achiga's about to play into that hand.</i>

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.090
<i>I feel bad for her, 
but there's nothing I can do.</i>

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:30.530
<i>What should I do?</i>

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:37.010
<i>Onee-chan, what should I do?</i>

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:45.440
Ron.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.170
10,200.

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:57.320
The sixth consecutive win!

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:02.240
She might not have won 
if it had been the 3 of circles.

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:03.860
Kuro-chan.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:04.960
Kuro.

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:09.440
The champion Miyanaga Teru!

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.180
She hasn't let anyone win a hand

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:13.020
since we've entered the 
second half of this match!

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:15.470
She is really stealing the show!

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:17.750
It's bonus hand 3 of East Hand 4

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:21.170
and she has a 100,000 point 
lead on the next player!

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:23.790
She's strong! She's too strong!

00:17:23.790 --> 00:17:27.860
This is the most powerful 
high-school player, Miyanaga Teru!

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.570
I think she's pretty exhausted.

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:37.560
I can imagine, I mean,

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.510
even if she looks one turn ahead,

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:43.250
her opponent wins no matter what.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:47.160
I've never seen Toki be so proactive.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:48.700
So that means that even then,

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:51.170
Miyanaga Teru can't be stopped?

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:52.250
Toki...

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.830
<i>How long will the champion's 
winning streak last?</i>

00:17:58.640 --> 00:17:59.680
<i>Ryuuka.</i>

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:02.150
Toki...

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:04.990
Hey, hey, Toki.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:10.060
You said you can see 
one turn ahead, right?

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:12.000
What if everyone else took

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.890
a really long time to 
think for their turn,

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:18.010
could you still see one turn ahead?

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:20.580
Yeah, usually.

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.700
What if it went by 
really, really quickly.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.740
Could you see two turns ahead?

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:26.890
Nope.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:29.450
Oh. That's too bad.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:30.990
Yeah.

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:35.130
But sometimes, I almost 
feel like I can see it.

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:37.620
Two turns ahead.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:39.130
Come on.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.460
When I've already seen one turn ahead

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:44.890
and I really want to see the next turn,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:49.850
if I concentrate hard, 
I get a tiny glimpse.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.240
Really?

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:55.530
Maybe I should try it at the club today,

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.900
see if I can do something 
that strange or not.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:02.610
Nah, being able to see one turn 
ahead already feels like cheating.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:03.990
Yeah, huh?

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:08.910
<b>Mahjong Club</b>

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.840
<i>I'm going to get a dora next turn.</i>

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:20.440
<i>All right.</i>

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:21.890
<i>Seeing one more turn ahead.</i>

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:32.280
<i>I'm seeing...</i>

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.190
<i>Ryuuka's upper thighs...</i>

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:37.320
<i>Why are they upside down?</i>

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:38.320
<i>Huh?</i>

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.490
Toki? Toki!

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.710
Toki! Hey, Toki!

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:51.540
<i>Ryuuka.</i>

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:54.290
<i>Is this a dream?</i>

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:06.430
I guess it's not a dream.

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.930
Couldn't you figure that 
out some other way?

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:14.090
Thank god.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:15.660
It looks like it's nothing serious.

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:18.160
I was worried you'd have to 
check into the hospital again.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:21.750
I've caused you guys 
so much trouble, again.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:23.320
I'm really sorry.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.580
There's nothing to apologize for.

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:33.290
Do you think you fainted because 
you tried to do that thing?

00:20:34.580 --> 00:20:35.740
Probably.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:36.460
What?

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:40.090
I just tried to double 
seeing one turn ahead.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:42.860
When I tried to see two turns forward,

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:45.220
I suddenly lost conciousness.

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:54.060
Toki, you can't ever 
try to do that again.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.070
Come on, I want you to promise.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.280
Okay.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:08.230
<i>I've never told Ryuuka, 
but ever since then,</i>

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:11.830
<i>I've been practicing at home in secret.</i>

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:15.640
<i>Yeah, sometimes I would faint.</i>

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:19.200
<i>We're in bonus round 3 of East Hand 4!</i>

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.320
<i>The dealer is none other than 
Shiraitodai's first player, Miyanaga Teru!</i>

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:25.380
<i>The champion's on an
overwhelming winning streak.</i>

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:28.110
<i>So far, the other three schools</i>

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.300
<i>have only stopped her streaks once!</i>

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.910
<i>She is now close to 100,000	
points ahead of Senriyama,</i>

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:35.920
<i>the school ranked second in the country.</i>

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.520
<i>We can only imagine what's 
on Senriyama's ace player,</i>

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.400
<i>Onjouji Toki's, mind!</i>

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:47.100
<i>Maybe I can handle it 
just once, right now.</i>

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.370
<i>I'm sorry, Ryuuka.</i>

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:55.490
<i>Double.</i>

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:57.320
<i>Two turns in the future!</i>

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.760
It's about to leave!

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.060
Hurry up!

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:29.930
Yuuki, wait!

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:31.980
Saki-chan, hurry, hurry!

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:33.850
I'm sorry.

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:42.050
We let one slip by.

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:43.970
Now we'll have to wait 30 
minutes for the next one.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.620
It won't take that long.

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:49.070
We're not in Nagano.

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:50.420
Oh, yeah.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:51.570
You're right.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:53.990
Jeez, that's the problem with you bumpkins.

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.820
Like you're one to talk.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:19.890
<b>Determination</b>

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:07.200
Shizu, drink this.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:08.130
Huh? Is this tea?

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:10.490
Yeah! It has caffeine, so if 
you drink it before a match,

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:12.470
you'll be more alert, and it has no 
calories, so it won't make you fat!

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:15.220
In fact, it helps to burn fat, so it 
could even help you lose weight!

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:17.660
By the way, it's called "Earl Gray," 
derived from an English nobleman who...

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:19.420
God, you and your trivia!

00:24:19.890 --> 00:25:00.000
<b>Watch Next Week</b>
